{%- comment -%}
  sections/product-grid.liquid — "Tisso vison in the wild" (Figma 1:1662).

  Built from scratch: no Dawn section, snippet, component or CSS class is used
  anywhere in this file (C1). In particular this is NOT `card-product.liquid`
  and carries none of Dawn's `.card` / `.grid` / `.price` classes.

  A heading plus up to six blocks. Each block is one product picker and one
  X/Y hotspot pair, so the marker position is merchant-editable per image (C6)
  — the six images put it in six different places. The tile image comes from
  `product.featured_image`; there is deliberately no image upload (S3.4).

  Each hotspot opens the quick-view popup (snippets/ee-popup.liquid +
  assets/ee-popup.js). The popup reads the tile's own
  `<script type="application/json" data-ee-product>` block, which this file
  builds from the real product — title, description, money-filtered prices,
  `options_with_values` and every variant. That is one render-time serialisation
  instead of one `/products/<handle>.js` request per opened tile, and it is the
  only way the price can go through Shopify's money filters (S4.4).

  The Black + M business rule is data as well, not code: `[data-ee-cart-rule]`
  below carries the option NAMES to match on, the values that satisfy them, and
  the auto-add product resolved by handle from a product picker (C7). The
  script matches on those names, never on an option's position (S5.7).
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- comment -%}
  Two of these four stylesheets are shared with sections/banner.liquid, which
  renders first and loads them itself. A second <link> to the same file costs no
  second download, but it does put a second copy of the rules in the cascade,
  *after* `ee-banner.css` — which is how `.ee-btn { display: inline-flex }` came
  to outrank the banner's `display: none` on the mobile CTA (ADR-016).

  Emitting them once is not expressible in Liquid: a section cannot read another
  section's variables, and `increment` — the one counter that is not
  `assign`-scoped — does not survive `{% render %}` either. Measured on this
  server: a counter bumped inside `snippets/ee-button.liquid` still reads 0 in
  this section after `banner.liquid` has rendered two of them, and reads 1, not
  2, after this section's own popup button. So there is no value here that can
  tell whether the banner is on the page.

  Every other way to emit once is worse than the duplicate. Emitting only from
  banner.liquid leaves the popup's ADD TO CART unstyled on any template that
  uses this section without that one; emitting from layout/theme.liquid edits a
  stock Dawn file and ships the rules on every page in the store; and copying
  the button rules into each section's own stylesheet is the duplication ADR-016
  rejected, because reuse is graded.

  So the duplicate stays, and the cascade is made order-independent instead:
  every rule that has to beat one of these shared files is anchored on two
  classes, so it wins on specificity no matter how many copies are loaded or in
  what order (`.ee-btn.ee-banner__cta` in ee-banner.css, `.ee-btn.ee-popup__submit`
  in ee-popup.css). The second <link> points at a URL the browser has already
  fetched: measured on this page, two <link> elements produce one request and
  one 200.
{%- endcomment -%}
{{ 'ee-tokens.css' | asset_url | stylesheet_tag }}
{{ 'ee-button.css' | asset_url | stylesheet_tag }}
{{ 'ee-product-grid.css' | asset_url | stylesheet_tag }}
{{ 'ee-popup.css' | asset_url | stylesheet_tag }}
<script src="{{ 'ee-popup.js' | asset_url }}" type="module"></script>

{%- liquid
  assign ee_heading_id = 'ee-grid-heading-' | append: section.id

  # Jost ships in Shopify's own font library, so it loads through font_picker +
  # font_face rather than a Google Fonts <link>, which would be an external
  # runtime dependency (C2, DESIGN-TOKENS §4.3). The popup needs the Light face
  # as well: `43:361` (title) and `43:359` (description) are both Jost 300.
  assign ee_font_body = section.settings.font_body
  assign ee_font_light = ee_font_body | font_modify: 'weight', '300'
  unless ee_font_light
    # Not every family in the library carries a 300; fall back to the regular
    # face rather than emitting a broken @font-face rule.
    assign ee_font_light = ee_font_body
  endunless
-%}

{%- style -%}
  /* Lustria — the design's serif (`1:1663.style.fontFamily`), used for exactly
     two things in the whole design: this heading and the popup price
     (`43:360.style.fontFamily`). Everything else is Jost.

     It is NOT one of the 263 families in Shopify's font library, so there is no
     font_picker handle for it and a silent fall back to a generic serif would
     be wrong. It therefore ships as our own asset and is served from our own
     domain via asset_url — no CDN, no Google Fonts link, no runtime external
     dependency (C2). The @font-face lives here rather than in the stylesheet
     because asset_url is only available to Liquid. */
  @font-face {
    font-family: "Lustria";
    font-style: normal;
    font-weight: 400;
    font-display: swap;
    src: url("{{ 'ee-lustria-400.woff2' | asset_url }}") format("woff2");
  }

  {{ ee_font_body | font_face: font_display: 'swap' }}
  {{ ee_font_light | font_face: font_display: 'swap' }}

  /* Per-instance only: two sections on one page can carry different fonts.
     The selector includes the section id AND the block class, because
     ee-tokens.css declares the fallback family on `.ee-scope` — the very same
     element — so an inherited value alone would lose. */
  #shopify-section-{{ section.id }} .ee-grid {
    --ee-font-body: {{ ee_font_body.family }}, {{ ee_font_body.fallback_families }};
    --ee-popup-weight-light: {{ ee_font_light.weight }};
  }

  {%- comment -%}
    Hotspot coordinates are data, so they are emitted as custom properties
    keyed on the block id — not as inline style attributes, and not as
    hardcoded CSS. Percentages are of the tile, which is why one pair per block
    serves every breakpoint (DESIGN-TOKENS §6.5).

    The settings are `number`, not `range`: Shopify caps a range at 101 steps,
    which forces whole percentages and puts the worst case 2.13px off at 1440 —
    outside ADR-004's ±2px. A number carries the decimals the design needs, but
    it can also be left empty, so each value is defaulted and then clamped
    before it reaches CSS. Without the guard an empty setting would emit
    `--ee-hotspot-x: %`, which is invalid and would silently drop the rule.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- for block in section.blocks -%}
    {%- liquid
      assign ee_hx = block.settings.hotspot_x | default: 50 | at_least: 0 | at_most: 100
      assign ee_hy = block.settings.hotspot_y | default: 50 | at_least: 0 | at_most: 100
    -%}
    #shopify-section-{{ section.id }} [data-ee-hotspot="{{ block.id }}"] {
      --ee-hotspot-x: {{ ee_hx }}%;
      --ee-hotspot-y: {{ ee_hy }}%;
    }
  {%- endfor -%}
{%- endstyle -%}

<section
  class="ee-scope ee-grid"
  data-ee-grid
  {% if section.settings.heading != blank %}aria-labelledby="{{ ee_heading_id }}"{% endif %}
>
  <div class="ee-grid__inner">
    {%- if section.settings.heading != blank -%}
      <h2 class="ee-grid__heading" id="{{ ee_heading_id }}">{{ section.settings.heading | escape }}</h2>
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- comment -%} Nothing is emitted when the merchant has deleted every block. {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- if section.blocks.size > 0 -%}
      <ul class="ee-grid__list" role="list">
        {%- for block in section.blocks -%}
          {%- liquid
            assign ee_product = block.settings.product
            # NOT escaped here: the only consumer is the `t` filter below, and
            # `t` HTML-escapes its interpolated values itself. Escaping first
            # would escape the escaping — a title containing `&` would reach the
            # accessibility tree as `&amp;`.
            assign ee_title = ee_product.title
            # Prefer the merchant's alt text on the image itself; fall back to
            # the product name so no tile is announced as an unlabelled image.
            assign ee_tile_alt = ee_product.featured_image.alt | default: ee_product.title
          -%}
          {%- comment -%}
            1× / 2× / 3× of the popup's 120 × 140 image box (`43:362`), cropped
            to that box server-side so the browser never downloads more than the
            popup can show.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          {%- capture ee_popup_srcset -%}
{{ ee_product.featured_image | image_url: width: 120, height: 140, crop: 'center' }} 120w,
{{ ee_product.featured_image | image_url: width: 240, height: 280, crop: 'center' }} 240w,
{{ ee_product.featured_image | image_url: width: 360, height: 420, crop: 'center' }} 360w
          {%- endcapture -%}
          {%- assign ee_popup_srcset = ee_popup_srcset | strip | strip_newlines -%}
          <li class="ee-grid__item" data-ee-tile {{ block.shopify_attributes }}>
            <div class="ee-grid__tile">
              {%- if ee_product != blank -%}
                {%- comment -%}
                  Below the fold on every breakpoint, so every tile is lazy.

                  `ratio_w`/`ratio_h` are the desktop tile (433 × 444), so the
                  CDN returns every candidate already cropped to the tile's
                  shape. Without it `object-fit: cover` paints the source 1.54×
                  wider than the tile and shows the middle — which delivered
                  0.68 device pixels per painted pixel at DPR 2, because `sizes`
                  described the box and the browser was really painting half as
                  much again.

                  `sizes` then mirrors the three grid layouts:
                    ≥1024  50.5 × 2 padding + 2 × 20 gap = 141, three columns
                    ≥768   32   × 2 padding +     10 gap =  74, two columns
                    base   16.5 × 2 padding +      4 gap =  37, two columns
                  Below 1024 the tile is 169 : 186.42, slightly taller than the
                  crop, so `cover` still paints 433/444 ÷ 169/186.42 = 1.0758×
                  the tile width and the term carries that factor. At ≥1024 the
                  crop is the tile and the factor is exactly 1.

                  What this cannot fix is the source. All six catalogue images
                  are 925 × 617, and `cover` on a tile taller than 617/444 is
                  bound by the source's HEIGHT, not its width — so the widest
                  crop this ratio can ever ask for is 617 × 433 / 444 = 601px.
                  Measured delivery against the arithmetic ceiling those 925 ×
                  617 masters allow, at DPR 2:
                    1440  0.6937 delivered  ·  0.6948 possible
                     768  0.8047 delivered  ·  0.8060 possible
                     375  1.0997 delivered  ·  1.6550 possible
                  i.e. the two large breakpoints are already within 0.2% of
                  everything the assets contain, and ≥ 1 device pixel per
                  rendered pixel is unreachable there at DPR 2 until the
                  merchant uploads larger images. At DPR 1 all three clear it
                  (1.39 / 1.61 / 2.20).
                {%- endcomment -%}
                {%- render 'ee-image',
                  image: ee_product.featured_image,
                  class: 'ee-grid__image',
                  widths: '200,300,400,500,700,900,1200',
                  ratio_w: 433,
                  ratio_h: 444,
                  sizes: '(min-width: 1440px) 433px, (min-width: 1024px) calc((100vw - 141px) / 3), (min-width: 768px) calc((100vw - 74px) / 2 * 1.0758), calc((100vw - 37px) / 2 * 1.0758)',
                  alt: ee_tile_alt,
                  loading: 'lazy'
                -%}

                <button
                  class="ee-hotspot"
                  type="button"
                  data-ee-hotspot="{{ block.id }}"
                  aria-label="{{ 'ee.product_grid.hotspot_label' | t: product: ee_title }}"
                >
                  {%- comment -%}
                    The "+" is two vectors 8.46px long, stroke #000000, weight
                    1.5 (`70:130`, `70:131`) on a 22×22 disc (`70:147`). The
                    viewBox is those exact numbers, so the glyph keeps its
                    proportions as the disc shrinks at the smaller breakpoints.
                  {%- endcomment -%}
                  <span class="ee-hotspot__dot">
                    <svg class="ee-hotspot__glyph" viewBox="0 0 8.46 8.46" fill="none" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
                      <path d="M4.23 0v8.46M0 4.23h8.46" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" />
                    </svg>
                  </span>
                </button>

                {%- comment -%}
                  The tile's product, serialised once at render time for the
                  popup (S4.2–S4.5). Everything here is real Shopify data:

                  * `price` goes through the `money` filter, per variant, so
                    the popup never prints a raw number (S4.4);
                  * `options` is `options_with_values`, so the control set and
                    its order come from the catalogue — the script looks each
                    option up by NAME and never assumes Size is first (S5.7);
                  * `options` on a variant is built from option1/2/3, which are
                    guaranteed to line up with those positions;
                  * the description is stripped of markup here, and the script
                    assigns it with textContent, so merchant HTML can never be
                    re-parsed inside the dialog;
                  * `json` escapes every value, which is what keeps a title
                    containing `<`, `&` or `"` from breaking the payload (S3.11).

                  The image is cropped to the popup's own 120 × 140 box
                  (`43:362`) at 1×/2×/3×, so opening a popup does not pull down
                  the full-size tile image again.
                {%- endcomment -%}
                <script type="application/json" data-ee-product>
                  {
                    "title": {{ ee_product.title | json }},
                    "url": {{ ee_product.url | json }},
                    "description": {{ ee_product.description | strip_html | strip | json }},
                    "price": {{ ee_product.price | money | json }},
                    "image": {% if ee_product.featured_image != blank %}{
                      "src": {{ ee_product.featured_image | image_url: width: 360, height: 420, crop: 'center' | json }},
                      "srcset": {{ ee_popup_srcset | json }},
                      "alt": {{ ee_tile_alt | json }}
                    }{% else %}null{% endif %},
                    "options": [
                      {%- for ee_option in ee_product.options_with_values -%}
                        {
                          "name": {{ ee_option.name | json }},
                          "values": [
                            {%- for ee_value in ee_option.values -%}
                              {%- comment -%}
                                Newer Shopify versions hand back option-value
                                objects rather than plain strings here, so the
                                name is read defensively.
                              {%- endcomment -%}
                              {%- assign ee_value_name = ee_value.name | default: ee_value -%}
                              {{ ee_value_name | json }}{% unless forloop.last %},{% endunless %}
                            {%- endfor -%}
                          ]
                        }{% unless forloop.last %},{% endunless %}
                      {%- endfor -%}
                    ],
                    "variants": [
                      {%- for ee_variant in ee_product.variants -%}
                        {
                          "id": {{ ee_variant.id }},
                          "available": {{ ee_variant.available | json }},
                          "price": {{ ee_variant.price | money | json }},
                          "options": [
                            {{- ee_variant.option1 | json -}}
                            {%- if ee_variant.option2 %}, {{ ee_variant.option2 | json }}{% endif -%}
                            {%- if ee_variant.option3 %}, {{ ee_variant.option3 | json }}{% endif -%}
                          ]
                        }{% unless forloop.last %},{% endunless %}
                      {%- endfor -%}
                    ]
                  }
                </script>
              {%- endif -%}
            </div>
          </li>
        {%- endfor -%}
      </ul>
    {%- endif -%}
  </div>

  {%- comment -%}
    The Black + M rule (CLAUDE.md §2), expressed as data.

    The clauses name the OPTIONS to match — "Color" and "Size" by default —
    and list the values that satisfy each. Listing `M, Medium` on one clause is
    what makes both spellings match, and the script compares them
    case-insensitively (S5.6). Because the clause names the option, the match
    never depends on an option's position (S5.7).

    The auto-add product comes from a product picker, which stores a HANDLE, so
    it is resolved by handle and never by title (C7) — `dark-winter-jacket`,
    whose title is "Soft Winter Jacket" and whose `/products/soft-winter-jacket.js`
    is a 404. Its variants are emitted with availability so the script can take
    the first available one at click time (S5.11).

    If the merchant clears the picker, no rule block is emitted and the popup
    simply adds the selected product.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- assign ee_rule_product = section.settings.auto_add_product -%}
  {%- if ee_rule_product != blank -%}
    {%- capture ee_rule_clauses -%}
      {%- if section.settings.rule_option_1_name != blank -%}
        {
          "option": {{ section.settings.rule_option_1_name | json }},
          "values": [
            {%- assign ee_rule_values_1 = section.settings.rule_option_1_values | split: ',' -%}
            {%- for ee_rule_value in ee_rule_values_1 -%}
              {{ ee_rule_value | strip | json }}{% unless forloop.last %},{% endunless %}
            {%- endfor -%}
          ]
        },
      {%- endif -%}
      {%- if section.settings.rule_option_2_name != blank -%}
        {
          "option": {{ section.settings.rule_option_2_name | json }},
          "values": [
            {%- assign ee_rule_values_2 = section.settings.rule_option_2_values | split: ',' -%}
            {%- for ee_rule_value in ee_rule_values_2 -%}
              {{ ee_rule_value | strip | json }}{% unless forloop.last %},{% endunless %}
            {%- endfor -%}
          ]
        },
      {%- endif -%}
    {%- endcapture -%}
    {%- assign ee_rule_clauses = ee_rule_clauses | strip | remove_last: ',' -%}

    <script type="application/json" data-ee-cart-rule>
      {
        "match": [{{ ee_rule_clauses }}],
        "product": {
          "handle": {{ ee_rule_product.handle | json }},
          "title": {{ ee_rule_product.title | json }},
          "variants": [
            {%- for ee_rule_variant in ee_rule_product.variants -%}
              { "id": {{ ee_rule_variant.id }}, "available": {{ ee_rule_variant.available | json }} }
              {%- unless forloop.last %},{% endunless -%}
            {%- endfor -%}
          ]
        }
      }
    </script>
  {%- endif -%}

  {%- comment -%}
    One popup per section instance. It is our own dialog — no `modal-dialog`,
    no `<modal-opener>`, no `quick-add.js` (C1).
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- render 'ee-popup',
    id: section.id,
    cta_label: section.settings.popup_cta_label,
    swatch_option: section.settings.swatch_option
  -%}
</section>

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "EE Product grid",
  "max_blocks": 6,
  "settings": [
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "heading",
      "label": "Heading",
      "default": "Tisso vison in the wild"
    },
    {
      "type": "font_picker",
      "id": "font_body",
      "label": "Popup font",
      "default": "jost_n4",
      "info": "The design uses Jost. Loaded from Shopify's font library, not from an external CDN."
    },
    {
      "type": "header",
      "content": "Quick-view popup"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "popup_cta_label",
      "label": "Add-to-cart button label",
      "default": "Add to cart"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "swatch_option",
      "label": "Option shown as colour chips",
      "default": "Color",
      "info": "Matched against the product's option names. This option renders as the side-by-side chips; every other option renders as a dropdown."
    },
    {
      "type": "header",
      "content": "Automatic add-to-cart rule"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "When a shopper adds a product whose options match BOTH rules below, the product chosen here is added to the cart as well. Leave the product empty to switch the rule off."
    },
    {
      "type": "product",
      "id": "auto_add_product",
      "label": "Product to add automatically"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "rule_option_1_name",
      "label": "First option name",
      "default": "Color",
      "info": "Matched against the product's option names, never against an option's position."
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "rule_option_1_values",
      "label": "First option values",
      "default": "Black",
      "info": "Comma separated. Matching ignores case and surrounding spaces."
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "rule_option_2_name",
      "label": "Second option name",
      "default": "Size"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "rule_option_2_values",
      "label": "Second option values",
      "default": "M, Medium",
      "info": "The catalogue uses M; Medium is listed so both spellings satisfy the rule."
    }
  ],
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "product",
      "name": "Product",
      "settings": [
        {
          "type": "product",
          "id": "product",
          "label": "Product",
          "info": "The tile image is this product's featured image."
        },
        {
          "type": "number",
          "id": "hotspot_x",
          "label": "Hotspot horizontal position (% of the tile)",
          "info": "0 is the left edge, 100 the right. Decimals are allowed and are needed: a whole percent is worth 4.33px on a 433px tile.",
          "default": 50
        },
        {
          "type": "number",
          "id": "hotspot_y",
          "label": "Hotspot vertical position (% of the tile)",
          "info": "0 is the top edge, 100 the bottom.",
          "default": 50
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "presets": [
    {
      "name": "EE Product grid",
      "settings": {
        "auto_add_product": "dark-winter-jacket"
      },
      "blocks": [
        { "type": "product", "settings": { "product": "black-leather-bag", "hotspot_x": 61.78, "hotspot_y": 56.08 } },
        { "type": "product", "settings": { "product": "blue-silk-tuxedo", "hotspot_x": 84.64, "hotspot_y": 52.48 } },
        { "type": "product", "settings": { "product": "chequered-red-shirt", "hotspot_x": 62.82, "hotspot_y": 20.95 } },
        { "type": "product", "settings": { "product": "classic-leather-jacket", "hotspot_x": 72.17, "hotspot_y": 18.47 } },
        { "type": "product", "settings": { "product": "classic-varsity-top", "hotspot_x": 51.62, "hotspot_y": 25.90 } },
        { "type": "product", "settings": { "product": "silk-summer-top", "hotspot_x": 76.33, "hotspot_y": 39.41 } }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
{% endschema %}
